Vitiligo (Mild Non-Segmental)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Small chalk-white macules on hands or face, symmetrical, purely cosmetic impact

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-050
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of extreme psychological distress and fear of spreading systemic infection after noticing a symmetrical skin change.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate the sudden discovery of symmetrical, stark white skin patches. Immediate clinical review was necessary to differentiate a benign autoimmune process from severe bilateral fungal infection (e.g., Tinea) or a toxic cutaneous reaction, preventing severe ongoing panic prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I just noticed these matching white, dead-looking spots on both the backs of my hands. I think I've caught a terrible fungal infection or parasite.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a slow-progressing localized depigmentation that was previously unnoticed. Tonight, while washing their hands, the patient suddenly observed small chalk-white macules on hands or face, symmetrical in nature. The stark contrast of the lesions triggered an immediate, severe anxiety attack, characterized by hyperventilation and a deep fear that a contagious organism was "eating" the skin's color. The patient has been washing the hands obsessively with hot water and harsh soaps. Denies pain, itch, or systemic illness.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High aesthetic distress and acute anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, visibly anxious, continuously inspecting hands.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals well-demarcated, completely depigmented (chalk-white) macules on the dorsal aspect of both hands, exhibiting a mirrored, symmetrical pattern. The skin is soft, supple, and structurally intact. There is absolutely no scaling, raised borders, or central clearing. The skin shows mild erythema consistent with recent obsessive washing with hot water. No regional lymphadenopathy. The lesions are of purely cosmetic impact.
Environmental Observations: Sink area cluttered with harsh hand sanitizers and soaps.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current topical medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vitiligo (Mild Non-Segmental) with secondary acute health anxiety - Status: Stable cutaneous finding
Differentials:

Tinea Manuum (Fungal Infection) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of an active, red, scaling, and pruritic border. True vitiligo lacks any scale.

Post-Inflammatory Hypopigmentation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The macules being stark, chalk-white (depigmented) rather than simply lighter than surrounding skin (hypopigmented), and no history of a preceding severe rash or trauma in those exact spots.

Leprosy (Hansen's Disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact fine touch and pinprick sensation perfectly preserved within the white macules, and lack of thickened peripheral nerves.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe acute psychological distress secondary to the discovery of symmetrical, depigmented macules, heavily fearing a contagious parasitic or fungal infection. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to rule out an active, spreading infectious process. Bedside evaluation definitively established the lesions as classic non-segmental vitiligo—structurally normal skin lacking pigment, with intact sensation and zero scale. Providing immediate clinical reassurance to halt the patient's damaging obsessive washing and de-escalating the severe panic was strictly necessary, preventing an inappropriate late-night emergency department presentation for a purely cosmetic autoimmune finding.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-infectious, and non-contagious nature of the condition. Instructed the patient to immediately cease using harsh soaps and hot water.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a thick, soothing hand cream immediately and put away all harsh hand sanitizers. By stopping the aggressive washing, the reactive redness will fade rapidly, and you will see that the skin is completely smooth, healthy, and not infected in any way.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or dermatology appointment in 2-3 weeks to discuss the natural history of the condition and potential topical therapies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the white patches break open into painful, bleeding sores, if your hands become rapidly swollen and hot to the touch, or if you develop a severe, high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Non-segmental vitiligo is a harmless condition where the immune system clears pigment cells, often symmetrically on both sides of the body. It does not cause pain or physical damage to the skin's structure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the white patches become covered in a thick, crusty, or itchy scale, which may indicate a secondary issue.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The white spots are entirely harmless and are not caused by a fungus or parasite. You cannot wash them away. The redness and soreness you feel are purely from scrubbing your hands raw tonight.
Actionable Steps: Wash your hands gently with lukewarm water and a soap-free cleanser only. Apply broad-spectrum sunscreen to the backs of your hands daily, as the white skin has no natural protection against sunburn.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Vitiligo (benign loss of pigment). Exam shows NO fungal infection or skin damage. Stop washing with harsh soaps. Safe to rest. See GP routinely.

